// texteditor_bigfile_setup.cla: gated-UI-scenario-ONLY companion, compiled
// TOGETHER with examples/texteditor.cla (Task 6, mac-target-4c) -- never
// part of the acceptance app itself. Its sole job is to self-write a real
// >32,000-byte fixture file, entirely within the same boot the
// texteditor_bigfile scenario then opens it in.
//
// Hooked to `on App.launch` rather than `App.startEmpty`: per Ch7's
// Launch Order, `launch` always fires first, but `startEmpty` and
// `openDocument` are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE (a launch that carries a
// `launchdoc` line fires openDocument instead of startEmpty) -- and this
// scenario's whole point is to `launchdoc` the fixture this file writes,
// so the write must happen somewhere that still runs on that branch.
// `launch` is the only event that does.
//
// The written `text` (a local, uncapped variable -- Ch3: "a text has no
// fixed capacity") is built by `append`ing a short string in a loop far
// past the textview widget's own 32,000-byte clamp (RTUI_TE_MAX,
// runtime/mac/rt_ui.h) -- a plain `text` variable never touches that
// clamp; only a textview's own `.text` property does. `file.writeText`
// then transfers it verbatim (Ch12), producing a real file over the
// guard's threshold for openPath (examples/texteditor.cla) to reject.
//
// Content is 3,000 repeats of "lorem ipsum" + a CR (36,000 bytes, regular
// whitespace every 12 bytes) rather than one giant unbroken word: Task 2
// found a single pathological ~32k-byte word makes classic TextEdit's
// line-wrap effectively quadratic, hanging TECalText for minutes on real
// m68k hardware. `\n` is the string-escape spelling of CR (13, the Mac
// newline -- Ch2), not LF.
on App.launch {
    var t: text
    var i: int

    i = 0
    while i < 3000 {
        t.append("lorem ipsum\n")
        i = i + 1
    }
    file.writeText("texteditor-bigfile.txt", t)
}
